# ------------------------------------------------------------------ background
H("Background")
P("Journals advertise speed, and authors, funders and evaluators use a journal's past time to acceptance, usually a median or the share of papers accepted within a limit, to set expectations for the next paper. Reporting of such statistics is inconsistent and seldom accompanied by uncertainty ⟦phillips2025,bilalli2021,andersen2021⟧, and aggregator pages already offer planning tools built on historical medians ⟦manusights⟧. Whether a journal's own recent history is a calibrated forecast of the next paper, and how to correct it if not, is not settled by descriptive work on publication delay ⟦bjork2013,ellison2002,argiles2023,sebo2019,luwel2020,hadavand2024,himmelstein,hanson2024,horbach2020⟧. A registered plan for a PubMed-wide model of acceptance lag with a random training/test split exists ⟦dianovics_lifecycle⟧; its registration states that it was not preregistered, it was evaluated at MetaROR in April 2026, and we found no results in October 2026.")
P("The statistical problem is forecasting under distribution shift with delayed outcomes. At the time of forecast, the papers that have been accepted are a biased sample (slow papers are not yet accepted), and papers submitted next may differ from those accepted recently in volume, mix and editorial practice. Conformal methods give distribution-free coverage under exchangeability, and extensions handle shift by weighting or by adaptively updating the miscoverage level ⟦gibbs2021,barber2023,angelopoulos2023⟧; they presuppose that coverage feedback is available, whereas here an outcome becomes known only after the paper is accepted, up to a year later.")
P("We ask (RQ1) how much a journal's own history improves on pooled forecasts; (RQ2) how calibrated the simple empirical forecast is under a strictly out-of-time protocol, and how sensitive that conclusion is to the treatment of slow papers; (RQ3) whether a forecaster that estimates the cohort-to-cohort shift from earlier complete cohorts is better calibrated than empirical quantiles and than three standard corrections; (RQ4) whether the method transfers to journals of other publishers; and (RQ5) how reliable event probabilities such as 'accepted within 72 days' are. The study is empirical and makes no causal claims.")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ methods
H("Methods")
H("Data", 2)
P(f"Received and accepted dates are deposited in Crossref by Springer Nature, Wiley and Taylor & Francis. For each publisher we took the 1,000 ISSNs with most articles in 2023, merged print and online ISSNs and kept journals in which at least 80% of 40 random 2023 records carried both dates and the number of dated 2023 articles was 150–3,000 ({M['frame_journals']} entries, {M['n_journals']} distinct journals). All records published from 1 January 2021 to 20 October 2026 were downloaded. The estimand is D, days from received to accepted, conditional on acceptance. After cleaning (duplicate DOIs, D < 1 or > 1,460 days, acceptance more than seven days after first online publication, non-research items by title or fewer than five references), {M['n_records']:,} of {M['n_raw_dated']:,} records remained; median D was {M['D_quantiles']['0.5']:.0f} days. Journals were assigned to an imprint by the modal DOI prefix. External data were PubMed History received and accepted dates of {E['n_journals_attempted']} journals of other publishers (Elsevier, Cell Press, Oxford and Cambridge University Presses, PLOS and society journals; none from Springer Nature, Wiley, Taylor & Francis or MDPI), selected by a journal list fixed in the collection script before any records were fetched, and kept if at least 600 dated records were returned (a few journals on the list returned no dated records; {E['n_journals_kept_ge600']} journals kept; {E['n_clean_records']:,} clean records). PubMed entry date served as the first-online date.")
H("Forecasting protocol and the strict out-of-time rule", 2)
P("Origins are quarterly (Figure 1). At an origin t0 the visible set of a journal is its papers first online within the preceding 12 months and already received and accepted before t0. The target is the set of papers received in the following 91 days. A journal-origin is evaluated with at least 100 visible and 30 target papers. Durations are capped at 480 days for training and scoring (see Results for why the cap matters). A method at origin t0 may use for learning only earlier origins o whose targets were complete at t0, that is o + 91 + 480 days ≤ t0; with the data ending on 20 October 2026 the evaluation origins are 1 October 2023 to 1 January 2025 (six origins, with one to six earlier complete origins). This rule keeps outcomes that were not yet determined at t0 out of every learned parameter. It does not mean that every counted paper was observable at t0: about 1% of target papers in the learning cohorts had not yet been accepted or published at t0 (their durations are determined by the cap), and re-estimating τ² without them changed it by at most 5% at the first evaluation origin and less than 1% later (independent audit). The first Part D run used a 365-day cap and nine origins; it is reported alongside (see Order of events).")
fig(f"{ROOT}/figures/fig1_protocol_schematic.png", "Figure 1. Conceptual schematic (not data) of the out-of-time protocol: forecasts use only the visible training set at the origin and are scored against papers received afterwards; parameters use only earlier cohorts whose outcomes are complete.", 6.0)
H("Forecasters", 2)
P("All forecasts are for the 0.1, …, 0.9 quantiles of ln D. F0: empirical quantiles of the visible 12-month sample (Hazen definition ⟦hyndman1996⟧). F6: exponentially time-decayed quantiles (half-life 182.5 days). V: half-widths about the median multiplied by one constant (grid 1.000 to 1.400 in steps of 0.025) that gives 80% pooled coverage on earlier complete origins. R: F0 with quantile levels replaced through the empirical distribution of probability-integral-transform values on earlier complete origins. ACI: adaptive update of the miscoverage level of the F0 80% interval, α ← α + γ(0.20 − m), with γ = 0.05 and m the pooled miscoverage on each earlier complete origin in time order ⟦gibbs2021⟧; it yields an interval only. F2, publisher-pooled quantiles that ignore journal identity, is a deliberately weak baseline used to measure the value of journal information.")
P("CV0 (proposed). Let y_1, …, y_n be the visible ln-durations, s² their variance. The predictive distribution is G(q) = (1/n) Σ Φ((q − y_i)/h) with h² = τ² + s²/n; quantiles are found by bisection. The term s²/n is the sampling variance of the journal's location; τ² is the variance of the location shift between the visible sample and the next cohort. τ² is estimated per publisher from earlier complete origins by the method of moments, τ̂² = max(0, Var_j(Δ_j) − mean_j(s²_tr/n_tr + s²_te/n_te)), where Δ_j is the difference between the mean ln-duration of the realised target cohort and of the visible sample of journal-origin j. CV0 is thus the empirical forecast convolved with a normal location shift. Variants: CVb adds the publisher-mean shift of Δ to the visible sample; CVg uses one τ̂² for all publishers.")
H("Metrics, hypotheses and inference", 2)
P(f"Primary metrics: (a) the journal-mean absolute error of 80% interval coverage, |coverage − 0.80|; (b) mean pinball loss over nine quantile levels ⟦koenker1978,gneiting2007⟧; (c) the mean 80% interval score ⟦winkler1972⟧. Because a journal-origin has 30 or more target papers, the absolute coverage error has an irreducible sampling component. Under exchangeability (visible and target samples drawn from the same distribution, simulated by bootstrapping each visible sample at the observed sizes) its expectation is {pa(floor)} points; the binomial error of the target alone gives a lower bound of {pa(floor_bin)} points. We report errors relative to the simulated value. Hypotheses fixed before the run: HD1, CV0 has lower absolute coverage error than F0; HD2, CV0's pinball loss is not more than 0.5% above F0's; HD3, CV0's pooled signed coverage lies within ±1.5 points of 80%; HD4, CV0 has lower absolute coverage error than each of V, R and ACI (Holm adjusted); HD5, the shortfall predicted for each journal-origin from CV0's distribution with earlier-cohort τ̂², G(q90_F0) − G(q10_F0) − 0.80, correlates positively with the observed shortfall across journals; HD6, with parameters frozen from the frame, CV0 has lower absolute coverage error than F0 and V on external journals. Uncertainty used journal-cluster bootstraps (3,000 draws, journal averaged first), t intervals with 6 degrees of freedom on the means of the seven imprints, publisher-level sign checks, and a moving-block bootstrap (block length 2) over the ordered origins ⟦efron1979,field2007⟧.")
H("Order of events and amendments", 2)
P("The cycle-3 protocol (Part D), the external journal list and the code were committed to the project's git history before any Part D result existed; this is a pre-specification by the analyst, not a registered report. An arithmetic slip in the list of origins and a programming error in the F6 forecaster stopped runs before any result and were corrected; a hard-coded origin list in the external script was corrected before the external analysis was first completed, and that analysis was rerun once the collection of external records had finished. After the first complete run (cap 365 days; nine origins), the pre-specified HD5 failed and CV0 over-covered by 1.2 points. An exploratory sensitivity analysis then showed that F0's shortfall depends on the cap, because a capped value lies inside the interval of any journal whose upper quantile reaches the cap, so a short cap inflates coverage. The analysis was therefore repeated with a 480-day cap (amendment D-A2, committed before any cap-480 panel result), which is the primary analysis here; the cap-365 results are reported alongside. The choice of 480 days followed the cap sensitivity of F0, but the cap-365 outcomes (HD5 failed, CV0 over-covered) had been seen, and a 480-day cap had been used on the same frame data in an earlier cycle. The hypotheses are therefore pre-specified and the cap is a post-hoc design choice: we treat both caps as co-primary evidence and call a result confirmed only where it holds at both. Analyses after the review of the manuscript (the exchangeable-null floor, paper-weighted coverage, win/tie/loss counts, a τ² = 0 ablation, Brier-score intervals, the external outlier sensitivity and the external run at cap 365) are post hoc and labelled as such. Earlier cycles of this project are documented in the repository; their results on dependence, article-level covariates and drift are not repeated here.")
H("Use of AI", 2)
P("The analysis code, the package and drafts of this manuscript were produced with an AI system (Claude, Anthropic) acting as an autonomous research assistant in a sandboxed environment; separately launched instances of the same model family performed adversarial reviews, a prior-art search and reproducibility audits. No human expert has reviewed the work.")
