H("Journal history has value, and the shortfall of empirical intervals (RQ1, RQ2)", 2)
P(f"Between-journal differences explained {pc(vc['sigma2_journal']/tot)} of the variance of ln D (95% CI {pc(vci['icc_journal'][0])}–{pc(vci['icc_journal'][1])}); {pc(vc['sigma2_resid']/tot)} lay between articles within a journal. On the two original origins ({M['n_journal_origins']:,} journal-origins, {M['n_journals_forecast']} journals, {M['n_target_papers']:,} target papers; 182-day targets), journal-specific empirical forecasts lowered pinball loss by {pc(fm['F0']['skill'])} relative to publisher-pooled forecasts, and their 80% intervals covered {pc(0.8+fm['F0']['cov80_minus'])} of later papers (journal average), {pt(fm['F0']['cov80_minus'])} points from nominal. In the strict six-origin analysis ({S['n_journals']} journals, {S['n_journal_origins']:,} journal-origins, {S['n_target_papers']:,} papers) F0 gave {pt(sm['F0']['signed_cov']['est'])} points (journal-cluster CI {ci(sm['F0'])}); paper-weighted it was {pt(X['paper_weighted_signed_F0'])} points; by origin the shortfall ranged from {pt(max(S['by_origin_signed_cov']['F0'].values()))} to {pt(min(S['by_origin_signed_cov']['F0'].values()))} points, and it was negative in all three publishers ({pt(X['by_publisher']['F0']['Springer Nature']['signed'])} Springer Nature, {pt(X['by_publisher']['F0']['Wiley']['signed'])} Wiley, {pt(X['by_publisher']['F0']['Taylor & Francis']['signed'])} Taylor & Francis).")
P(f"The size of the shortfall depended strongly on the treatment of slow papers (Figure 2a). At origins where every setting had complete targets, F0's signed coverage error with 91-day targets was {pt(cap(270,91)['est'])} points with a 270-day cap, {pt(cap(365,91)['est'])} with 365, {pt(cap(480,91)['est'])} with 480 and {pt(cap(1460,91)['est'])} with no cap, whereas 182-day target cohorts gave the same values within 0.2 points. A cap sets slow papers to the cap value, which lies inside the interval of every journal whose upper quantile reaches it. Evaluations of turnaround forecasts on capped or censored durations can therefore overstate calibration; we use a 480-day cap, whose estimate differs from the 1,460-day cap by about {abs(cap(480,91)['est'] - cap(1460,91)['est'])*100:.1f} points (the intervals overlap), and report the 365-day results as co-primary. This sensitivity clips training and target durations together, so it does not separate their effects, and the 1,460-day setting is right-censored at the end of the data (a few tenths of a point at most).")
